r  disaffected europeans to plant flags and  finally call dibs on their own property  this was in stark contrast to the  situation back home as historian gordon  s wood wrote land was scarce in relation  to people and therefore was expensive  hence unable to afford their own land to  farm europeans were compelled to work  for others either by becoming laborers  for landowners in the countryside or  more often by migrating to the cities to  engage in manufacturing goods and  factories because it was easy to find  people to fill both kinds of jobs  wages sucked as a result a lot of  underpaid non-property-owning european  workers put off marriage and kids wow  putting off marriage and kids because  you can't make enough money that would  never happen to anyone i know  i wish i had kids they would love me but  i don't and even though the founding  fathers may have hated the oppressive  system they fled  they somewhat recreated it it's like how  you work your whole life not to become  your parents but you look in the mirror  one day and you realize you're just like  them under capitalism property ownership  does equal liberty at least to some  degree we want to live where everyone  else vacations homeownership in america  is the best way to build generational  wealth so if your family historically  did not have access to property for  reasons  you have a much smaller chance of upward  mobility than people whose families have  been able to use homes as assets equity  and inheritances having trouble paying  down a debt if you're a homeowner you  could refinance or take out a second  mortgage if you're not you can  i don't know like sell bodily fluids  according to the federal reserve the  median net worth for homeowners in 2019  was 250 000  compared to a paltry 6  300  for renters if you're a homeowner it's  also likely that you live around a lot  of other homeowners that means you and  your neighbors are paying property taxes  into the local schools that result in  better funding than neighborhoods with  lower income and higher proportions of  renters so your kids not only likely  benefit from generational wealth but  from a better education and what a cool  system where the quality of a child's  education is tied to property tax rates  in their neighborhood  i see no problems with this and let's  not forget the property ownership was  used for deliberately exclusionary  purposes  like restricting voting rights to  minorities and women in fact even white  men who didn't own property were often  disenfranchised from the voting process  until the mid 19th century as catherine  levine einstein and maxwell palmer point  out even today homeowners dominate  contemporary local politics they  participate at far higher rates are  electorally powerful and comprise  virtually all elected officials even in  cities with large majorities of renters  their disproportionate influence comes  with stark consequences for american  local governments land use and housing  policies that favor the interest of  homeowners have spurred high housing  costs sprawling and environmentally  destructive land use racial and economic  segregation and unequal access to high  quality public goods at the  constitutional convention the question  of whether you needed to own property to  participate politically was hotly  contested and folks like future  president james madison argued only land  owners could be trusted with our  delicate republic see in madison's mind  realistically most americans would never  own property and if all those unproperty  people banded together and say  voted in unison well that would be bad  news bears for the upper crust but like  what if we did that for real though  others at the convention like kite  enthusiast benjamin franklin disagreed  concerned that land ownership as a  condition of electoral participation  would create a powerful aristocracy and  powerless lower class besides many in  the latter group he pointed out had  fought 