 points out even if  the price zillow offered for your house  was lower than its market value the  convenience of the transaction and the  promise that the deal would go through  quickly was attractive enough to lure in  more sellers and grow zillow's inventory  in essence zillow was simply home  flipping a practice any hgtv viewer is  familiar with the asking price was 390  000 and we wound up paying 9 billion  plus closing costs  ideally zillow would buy homes and then  sell them for more than they purchased  them on top of that ibuyers bringing  money through transaction fees the real  cash cow in the deal zillow predicted  that their eye buying scheme which they  called zillow offers would rake in an  extra 20 billion dollars a year but  house flipping isn't that lucrative on a  small scale you can't say buy a house  for a hundred thousand and sell it for a  million the margin between buying costs  and selling profits is relatively small  so in order to live up to the massive  promise of a multi-billion dollar surge  in revenue zillow had to go big or go  home or go big  and get homes and that's exactly what  they did zillow snapped up 7 000 homes  at prices that baffled their competitors  and in many cases delighted homeowners  who were putting their houses on the  market zillow would sometimes pay more  than the price at which local real  estate agents had valued the homes in  order to entice the seller to make an  easy and quick decision and this  practice inspired a now viral tick tock  where real estate agent sean gotcher  posits why would they pay 340  for this 31st home whenever they've only  paid 300 for these others  well what that just did is create a new  comp so when they go to sell these other  30 homes  that extra 40 000 you could say this one  sold for 340  just made them 1.2 million of course  zillow tried to shoot down these  insinuations in a statement to yahoo  news claiming that we pay market value  for every home we purchase when we  looked 