ies and businesses are  priced out of their communities and  we're not alone in this america's maple  syrup soaked neighbors to the north are  experiencing something similar canadian  publication the walrus illustrated the  problem with the story of s.w welch an  independent used bookstore in montreal  when the building house in the bookstore  was bought by a real estate firm 68 year  old stephen welch was informed that the  store's rent taxes insurance and  maintenance would increase to 5 000 a  month a staggering 150 percent  unable to afford the surge welch said  simply i think they'd rather the  building be empty than have me here and  increasingly this is true throughout the  united states as well for a real estate  firm that can afford to buy up  properties in lucrative locations and  leave them empty it's a waiting game as  executive director of downtown montreal  business association glenn costinera  explained say a landlord buys a property  with a mom and pop shop that pays its  rent on time every month but it only  pays one thousand dollars the landlord  may think he can get five thousand or  ten thousand per month even if the  property remains vacant for 10 months it  only takes one month's rent with the new  tenant to make back what they lost  some cities have created taxes and fees  to penalize firms who leave usable  property vacant for extended periods of  time but real estate speculation the act  of buying a property in anticipation of  favorable changes in the market has  proven too profitable to be shut down  especially by slaps on the wrist like  oakland california's 6 000 a year flat  fee for homes in use fewer than 50 days  a year and in a red-hot housing market  some 16 million homes still remain  vacant throughout the united states the  injustice of there being 16 million  vacant homes in a country with half a  million unhoused people is pretty  staggering meanwhile since the recession  the value of american real estate has  ballooned at an unprecedented rate but  remember lots of homeowners lost their  houses during the crisis so instead of  these gains going to moderate or  middle-income homeowners it's going into  the coffers of huge corporations and  their shareholders and it's all fun  games and easy television for our  glowing surrogates and their newly  remodeled happily ever afters but when  you turn the tv off the reality is a lot  darker one survey referenced in fortune  showed that some 82 percent of  millennials have at least one  significant regret about buying a home  including decreasing home values  neighborhoods that turn out to be a bad  fit and the decidedly unglamorous  realities of fixer-uppers meanwhile  foreclosure spiked a staggering 700 in  january 2022 according to data company  black knight this picture doesn't quite  square with the cheery antics of  christina on the coast so what about the  majority of us left window shopping on  zillow fantasizing about the day we too  will join the ranks of landowners in  some ways we're cosplaying a dream in  stark contrast to reality put  differently we're situating ourselves  squarely within a system  that's rendered us pretty powerless iris  marion young contends that the goal of a  dream house sets workers working and  keeps workers working fearing job loss  working overtime the consumer-driven  desire of civic privatism tends to  produce political quietism that is to  say when we're really worried about  finding our own single-family house  we're gonna work our butts off instead  of questioning the political system that  puts us in this position as sociologist  david madden and urban planner peter  marcuzza write in their book in defense  of housing ownership restricts household  members opportunities to engage in other  activities as well as their desires to  do so especially their willingness to  participate in collective action that  might involve social conflict when we're  more focused on benefiting from a shitty  system we're less likely to agitate  against that system at the same time the  writers argue that this individualist  thinking means that if something goes  wrong the individual is thought to be to  blame rather than social and political  structures the fact that so many  millennials can't buy a house or can't  keep up with their mortgage payments  isn't because they're blowing they're  not keeping up with inflation wages on  avocado toast it's because they're being  priced out by huge corporations zillow's  accessibility belies that reality and  keeps us dreaming and that's what makes  it so seductive but what do you guys  think are you still going to spend your  friday nights on a hot date with the  zillow listings and a rerun of property  brothers or is real estate porn actually  a lot more sinister than the other twins  that do real estate might have you think  let us know in the comments huge thanks  to all our patrons and if you haven't  checked it out in a while check out our  patreon page we have a lot of cool new  stuff there um also check out our new  stream wisecrack live every thursday  right here hit that subscribe button  like you're clicking buy on the zillow  listing for the french riviera castle of  your dreams and don't forget to ring  that bell and as always thanks for  watching later  [Music]  you

